          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How can I tell from the tabular list that a code is a combination code rather than a plain one?",
          "answer": "In ICD-10-CM, combination codes are single codes that represent two conditions, or a condition and its complication. When a combination code exists, it should be used instead of coding the conditions separately, according to \"ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection\".\n\nOne way to identify when a combination code might be appropriate is by looking at the Alphabetic Index. If the index links two conditions with the word \"with,\" the classification presumes they are associated, leading to a combination code. For example, hypertension combined with heart or kidney disease often uses combination codes, as described in \"ICD-10-CM Chapter 9: diseases of the circulatory system (I00-I99)\".\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
